WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.594 --> 00:00:02.917
[fast breathing]

00:00:02.917 --> 00:00:05.260
[Chuck screaming]

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.610
Jamie Lee: I can't imagine
having to do that to myself.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.200
Hi, my name is Dr. Jamie,

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.160
and I'm an emergency medicine doctor

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.690
and content creator at TheStriveToFit.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.750
Working in the emergency
room is very fast-paced,

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.280
and you see serious complications

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.010
like heart attacks, strokes.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:22.540
Today, we're going to be looking at

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.750
some do-it-yourself
surgery scenes in movies

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.053
and TV shows and judging
how real they are.

00:00:30.554 --> 00:00:33.054
[tense music]

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.570
Drinking saltwater is not an antidote

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.760
to any of the poisons that I know of.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.310
However, it can help you throw up.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:44.750
If you have a lot of
liquid in your stomach,

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.500
trying to vomit the poison is
not an unreasonable approach.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.500
If he's already having
dizziness or unsteadiness,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.160
whether it's neurological
or cardiovascular effects

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.737
from the poison, it might
actually be too late.

00:00:56.737 --> 00:00:59.270
[dramatic music]

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.710
Him putting the pads on himself, as he is

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:05.930
in ventricular tachycardia,
that is exactly what I would do.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:08.910
Any signs of instability,
I would shock them.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.630
Typically we have one pad
on the front of the chest

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:14.100
and then another one on the back

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.500
so that when it delivers a
shock, it really is transmitting

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.290
the electrical shock through the heart.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.270
The pads that he put on himself
are actually pretty small,

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:23.950
so the ones that you see in the hospital

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.653
will be a little bigger than that.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.360
Our first priority is getting an IV access

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.520
so that we can deliver the medications.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.759
In the absence of having
direct access to his veins,

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:40.040
you know, your neck does
have a big jugular vein

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.910
that you can potentially have access to.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.890
Man: Do we know what it is yet?

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.329
Ventricular tachycardia -- digitalis.

00:01:46.329 --> 00:01:48.960
They said it was digitalis,
which is a heart medication.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.540
I think that was the poison.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:53.130
In case of digitalis, there's an antidote.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.380
It's called Digibind.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.576
Sometimes you know from history,

00:01:56.576 --> 00:01:58.770
and you can figure out what
the toxin is right away.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.190
Sometimes you have to send out some levels

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.469
and it can take a really long time.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:06.130
[digital keys clicking]

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:07.710
When you do deliver the shock,

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.910
you do see a little bit of a jolt.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.950
I think his was a little more
exaggerated, but it is there.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.420
Realistically, the treatment
to ventricular tachycardia

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.320
and arresting from that is being shocked.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.300
He did get shocked pretty quickly

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.650
after losing his pulses and flatlining,

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.240
so I think that would've helped anyone.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.280
Once you shock somebody back to life,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.380
they're still unstable,
because there's still toxin

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.470
and there's still poison inside his body.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.330
So you wouldn't expect them
to be functioning normally

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:38.810
and going about their day.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.460
But of course, he's James Bond.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.110
I'm going to give it a 3 out 10.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.403
Woman: Careful, careful.

00:02:45.403 --> 00:02:48.216
I really like this scene.

00:02:48.216 --> 00:02:49.049
Man: Ugh.

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:49.882
Wasp.

00:02:51.349 --> 00:02:52.182
Woman: What's that for?

00:02:52.182 --> 00:02:55.560
So he is performing an
emergency cricothyrotomy,

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.520
one of the more heroic procedures

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.250
that gets done sometimes
in emergency medicine.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.730
So, if somebody has a closed
airway from allergic reaction

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.640
or sometimes even smoke inhalation,

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.770
one of the ways that you can treat that is

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:13.110
by cutting a hole in your throat
and then putting a tube in

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.960
and ventilating the patient that way.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.760
I would've probably tried
to open the airway first

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.280
and see if there's anything else.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.900
They kind of jumped right into
cutting the neck, you know,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.023
which is usually our last
resort in cases like this.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.090
They actually put some alcohol on the neck

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.680
in an attempt to sterilize the area.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.650
So, CDC recommends that you use alcohol

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.970
that's at least 60% to
90% in concentration

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.950
in order to be able to
kill bacteria and viruses.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.220
It's not the best way
to sterilize instruments

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.270
or the surgical field, but
I think out in the field,

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.000
when you don't have access
to sterile equipment,

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.650
that's the best thing you can do.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.440
So for him to just kind of
plop the knife into the neck,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.340
I think that's a little unrealistic.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.170
There's a lot of important
structures in the neck.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:00.810
Make sure you are going
in the right place.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.879
So you really want to touch
and feel the neck anatomy

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:07.220
and feel the place that
where you're going to cut.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.800
And normally when we do the procedure,

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.180
we make one long vertical
incision on the neck

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.090
and then you feel the anatomy,

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.217
and then you can make a smaller incision

00:04:16.217 --> 00:04:18.020
for the tube to go into.

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:20.880
This is an emergency, you
know, your airway closes in

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.440
and you don't have a lot of time.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.510
The person is unable to breathe.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.580
In cases like this where the
throat literally closes in,

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.260
there's no space for you to be able

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:32.807
to be backing the patient or
even doing mouth-to-mouth.

00:04:32.807 --> 00:04:34.047
He's breathing, he's breathing.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:37.352
That's it.

00:04:37.352 --> 00:04:38.430
Also, if someone is not breathing

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.870
and someone is unconscious,
you want to make sure

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.690
that they have a pulse,
you want to make sure

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.830
that their heart is
beating, and if it's not,

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.700
you want to do chest compressions

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:48.500
to make sure that the blood is circulating

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.750
to the brain and all
the other vital organs.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.370
I'm going to give this 6 out of 10.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:54.953
A puncture wound.

00:04:56.571 --> 00:04:59.600
You have it deep -- nicked the artery.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.920
If the doctor hadn't
called it a deep wound

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.070
that nicked the artery,
I think the whole scene

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:08.300
would've been much more believable.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.480
There's a lot of big,
important vessels in your neck.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.580
If you nicked one of the
arteries in your neck,

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:15.950
that's a big deal.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.210
You're going to need surgery for that,

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.480
you don't want to just close
up the top and call it a day.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.080
Because the artery that's nicked

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.850
is going to continue to bleed.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.820
The doctor was doing simple
interrupted stitches,

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:31.010
which is individual stitches
to close up the wound.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:32.300
That's typically what I would do

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.100
if somebody had a superficial wound.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.090
It obviously takes some time,

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.990
because you're tying off
each individual stitches.

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:40.359
[dramatic music]

00:05:40.359 --> 00:05:45.359
What John Wick ends up doing
is a much faster stitch.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.570
What's called a running
stitch is what I'm imagining

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:51.210
he's doing, where you
don't tie off every stitch,

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.830
but you just keep going
throughout the entire length

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:54.950
of the wound.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:58.100
One downside of that is if
the stitch becomes loose,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.017
you basically expose the whole wound

00:06:00.017 --> 00:06:02.218
and it can become undone.

00:06:02.218 --> 00:06:05.170
[dramatic music]

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:07.510
It's very uncomfortable to
have to put a needle in,

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:11.260
a needle out, and tie off
your stitches without any kind

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.010
of lidocaine or anything like that.

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:16.510
But it's not very deep,
you're only getting the skin

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:19.430
with the needles, so even though
it would be uncomfortable,

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.750
it's not unreasonable
that you would do this

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:23.130
without local anesthetic.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:25.140
Doing it in the mirror,
I think, would take a lot

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:28.820
of hand-eye coordination, but
it would be possible to do it.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:30.638
Maybe a 3 out of 10.

00:06:30.638 --> 00:06:32.070
Man: What's the matter, you don't think

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:35.210
jockeying papers across a desk
is a noble effort for a cop?

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:39.320
Typically, I would not take
off the glass with my hands

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.560
because you really want to
avoid any additional injuries.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.180
You don't really know what
you're going to encounter

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.000
when you put your fingers in your wound.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.430
So using something like tweezers

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.767
or any instrument would
probably be better.

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:54.050
John McClane: Pull me down for 20.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:56.970
So there's a saying that goes, "Solution

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:58.850
to pollution is dilution."

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:03.040
Anytime there is dirt or
foreign body or wound,

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.280
you can use tap water to
kind of really flush it out.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.504
There's actually research that
shows how tap water is just

00:07:08.504 --> 00:07:10.703
as effective as using sterile saline.

00:07:14.203 --> 00:07:17.200
Once you take out some
of the bigger pieces

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.550
and you wrap it really well,
you should be able to walk

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:24.340
on it, unless there is some
ligamental or any bony injuries,

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:26.300
and that might make it harder to walk.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:28.520
But if you really had
to, you should be able

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.540
to walk on it and just hobble.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.500
I would give it 7 out of 10,

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.190
because I think he did the best he could

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.120
with the tools available to him,

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.896
which is just his hands and running water.

00:07:38.896 --> 00:07:41.235
[dramatic music]

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:43.560
No, listen to me, Logan.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.350
So he basically makes an
incision on his abdomen.

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.080
Not even on his chest.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.000
Has to put his hand
through and essentially go

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.140
through the diaphragm to
be able to reach the heart.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.840
And that's actually just a lot

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:58.760
of damage that you're
going to have to fix later.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:00.180
You're going to have difficulty breathing

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.620
that needs to be repaired.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:03.470
So, that is not the approach

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:08.202
that surgeons would normally
take to get to the heart.

00:08:08.202 --> 00:08:10.399
[machine beeping]

00:08:10.399 --> 00:08:13.450
Yeah, I mean, he just did
a major surgery on himself.

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:15.380
There's actually a
couple of famous surgeons

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:17.720
that did surgery on themselves.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:20.250
There's one that did surgery on himself

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:23.350
in Antarctica when he was doing research

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:26.230
and didn't have any
access to medical care,

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:29.063
and he had an appendicitis,

00:08:29.063 --> 00:08:30.640
and he knew it was going to go bad

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:32.200
unless he took the appendix out.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.150
So he actually performed the surgery

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:35.920
on himself in the '60s.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:38.800
I would say obviously these are rare cases

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.596
and they're exceptions.

00:08:40.596 --> 00:08:41.429
[machine beeping]

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:43.480
[heavy breathing]

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.130
You can see that his blood
pressure is very low.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:49.180
It's 50-something over 30-something.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:51.610
And you can hear the flatline beep,

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:55.040
which I feel like is used a
lot in movies and TV shows

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.930
to indicate that the person has died.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:58.990
Essentially means the
heart is not beating.

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:01.060
He actually flatlined for a little bit,

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:02.470
and he was still conscious

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:04.823
while the flatline beep was happening.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:10.720
So you wouldn't be awake
and saying somebody's name.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:13.150
You know, you would be
unconscious and then dying.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:14.340
1 out of 10.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.320
Just because it's so preposterous

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.360
that he's doing his own heart surgery

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.240
by going through his abdomen
and reaching in with his hand.

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:26.585
That's just crazy.

00:09:26.585 --> 00:09:28.350
[dramatic music]

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:32.690
It looks like the character
has a penetrating trauma

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:35.030
to her lower abdomen.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:37.810
It's a little unclear how
much bleeding she's having

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:39.410
from that wound itself.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:43.030
The extent of the damage
really depends on how deep

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:46.670
the glass went and what
structures it's affecting.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.410
Are there any major blood vessels?

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:52.090
Is it the bowel that's being
damaged from the glass?

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:54.910
That can lead to a lot of
different complications.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:57.700
So one of them would be
significant bleeding.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:09:58.870
That can lead to death.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:09:59.990
The other thing that I'd be worried

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:02.529
about is getting an infection.

00:10:02.529 --> 00:10:05.731
[heavy breathing]

00:10:05.731 --> 00:10:07.150
[fabric tearing]

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:08.730
When there's bleeding,
you want to put pressure

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:10.930
on the bleeding, so I think
she did the right thing

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:12.180
by wrapping herself.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:14.310
In her situation, where
she doesn't have access

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:16.500
to the hospital or medical care,

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.880
I think leaving that in
there could potentially

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:20.900
cause more damage just
because every time you bump

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.870
into something, it can
cause deeper damage.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:27.000
It is a wound that you
could potentially die from.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.240
Blood loss and the amount
of blood that you see

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.550
in TVs and movies, it's
always more jarring

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:36.880
and always really leaves a
lasting impact on your mind.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.140
But often as long as it is controlled,

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:42.070
bleeding is stopped, and
the injuries are fixed,

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:45.930
it's often OK to see a lot of blood.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.710
Your body will make up that lost blood.

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:51.723
This was maybe a 6 out of 10.

00:10:54.081 --> 00:10:57.670
We often cut patients' clothes off.

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:59.850
You want to make sure
there's no other injuries

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.390
to the body, but if they're able to walk

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:05.340
and stand on their own, we
usually just have patients take

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.130
off their pants instead of cutting them.

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:08.300
Because, you know, it's nice to be able

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:10.170
to save the clothing if you can.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.330
In his case, I think
maybe there's just a lot

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.540
of dried blood and maybe
it would've been painful

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:17.042
for him to, like, peel off the pants.

00:11:17.042 --> 00:11:21.120
I think the warm bath is just
to wash off the dried blood.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:24.320
We don't submerge wounds
in the emergency room.

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:26.820
I think that can be a nidus
for infection, probably.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:30.083
So you don't want to be soaking
in potentially dirty water.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:34.810
I really like what he's
doing with the saline bottle.

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:38.900
He mixed in Betadine and
basically made a solution

00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:40.950
that he can wash out his wound with.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:43.860
I actually often do that
with my saline bottles also.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:45.880
So if I don't have a spray cap,

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:48.160
I'll poke some holes on the cap

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:50.923
of the saline bottle to
make it into a spray bottle.

00:11:55.398 --> 00:11:58.207
He was injecting some lidocaine,

00:11:58.207 --> 00:11:59.370
which is a local anesthetic.

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:02.050
It would be hard to see,
and you probably have

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:03.830
to do a little bit of digging to visualize

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:06.100
all the fragments and foreign bodies.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:07.360
That's where the lidocaine comes

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:10.000
in really handy, because you
don't want to be digging in.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.140
That's really uncomfortable.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:14.780
With the lidocaine, then you
can do a little more exploring.

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:18.280
In cases of gunshot wounds,
we'll often take an X-ray

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:21.460
to see what fragments
are left inside the body.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:24.290
One fun thing that we
sometimes do is whenever

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:27.340
there's a bullet entry,
we'll tape a paper clip

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:29.426
to the wound site so that
when we take an X-ray

00:12:29.426 --> 00:12:31.500
we can know exactly where that is.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:33.870
So we can kind of determine
"Where's the entry wound

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:35.450
and where is the exit wound?"

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:37.670
This one, I thought he
did a really great job.

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:40.950
He also had access to a lot
of real medical equipments

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.903
and medication, so I
would give it 8 out of 10.

00:12:46.535 --> 00:12:49.960
Oh!

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:52.370
This scene is really difficult to watch.

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:55.860
He has a crush injury, and now he's making

00:12:55.860 --> 00:13:00.860
the difficult decision
to amputate his own arm.

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:03.340
[dramatic music]

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:06.950
He's applying essentially
a tourniquet to his arm.

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:09.930
The purpose of that is to
prevent the blood flow.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:11.910
There are big arteries that go

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:13.240
through your arm to supply blood

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:15.230
to your forearm and your hands.

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:17.290
And you don't want to go
through the whole pain

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.520
of amputating your own arm if you're going

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:22.660
to die from the blood loss.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:25.935
So you want to tightly
tie off the tourniquet

00:13:25.935 --> 00:13:26.768
so that it cuts off the blood supply

00:13:26.768 --> 00:13:27.601
to the rest of your distal arm.

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:32.510
He has a carabiner that he twists

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:34.960
on itself to make the
tourniquet even tighter,

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:36.060
and that's pretty similar

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.420
to what a real tourniquet looks like.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:40.430
There's a plastic part where you can twist

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:42.083
off to make it even tighter.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:44.303
[dramatic music]

00:13:44.303 --> 00:13:47.520
That is a very small knife
to be cutting your arm with.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:50.920
Typically, amputations are done
in a very controlled setting

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:54.480
in the operating room,
not in the emergency room.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:55.930
They probably have a bone saw,

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:57.830
rather than having to break your own arm.

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:00.360
Your muscle breaks down, and you can go

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:04.310
into rhabdomyolysis, and
you can damage your kidneys.

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:06.040
Your kidneys can shut down.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:08.336
You can have a systemic illness

00:14:08.336 --> 00:14:09.180
from a crush injury like this.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:12.230
So the longer he stays in that
position, the worse it is.

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:14.540
Not to mention the dehydration
that he's going through

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:16.450
because he doesn't have access to water.

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:17.970
So this is really just survival.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:19.887
It's life and death.

00:14:19.887 --> 00:14:21.220
[dramatic music]

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:25.590
I love the audio of what it feels like

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:27.630
to really aggravate that nerve.

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.480
If you've ever had a pinched nerve

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.280
in your elbow, you know it's

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:34.150
a very electrical-like,
zinging kind of pain.

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:36.305
So you can just imagine
directly touching the nerve

00:14:36.305 --> 00:14:38.430
and imagine what that feels like.

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:40.250
So I thought they did a really great job

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:42.050
combining the audio and the visual.

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:43.610
If it were me, because I know

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:45.560
the nerve is going to be more sensitive,

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:47.370
I would just cut it as fast as I can.

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.150
I think he's trying to overcome that

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:50.680
with his mental strength

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:53.210
of telling himself, "Don't
pass out, don't pass out."

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:56.150
If the tourniquet is on correctly,

00:14:56.150 --> 00:14:58.970
then hopefully the bleeding
will be a lot slower.

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:01.210
And hopefully it wouldn't be to the point

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:04.310
where he passes out and continues to bleed

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:06.530
and then basically dies from blood loss.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:08.650
You know, I think in terms
of passing out from pain,

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:10.180
that really depends on the person.

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:11.560
Everyone has a different threshold.

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:13.440
I thought they did a great job, actually.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.250
I would give this 9 out of 10.

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.236
And I'm sure part of that is because

00:15:18.236 --> 00:15:20.892
I know that this really happened.

00:15:20.892 --> 00:15:23.111
[waves lapping]

00:15:23.111 --> 00:15:23.944
Ah.

00:15:23.944 --> 00:15:25.730
You could see a lot of swelling,

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:28.380
and even his face looks a little
more swollen on that side.

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:30.019
There is probably a lot of inflammation

00:15:30.019 --> 00:15:32.440
and potentially an abscess underneath.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:33.420
It happens.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:35.580
People do from time to time come

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:39.200
to the emergency room
and often it's obvious

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.355
that the tooth just needs to come out.

00:15:41.355 --> 00:15:44.286
[heavy breathing]

00:15:44.286 --> 00:15:46.648
[metal clanking]

00:15:46.648 --> 00:15:48.530
I can't imagine having
to do that to myself.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:51.700
If you're using a skate
to take out the tooth,

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:53.890
you risk partially breaking the tooth

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:56.300
or injuring other structures around.

00:15:56.300 --> 00:16:00.250
Risky, but I think in his case
he had no access to dentists.

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:01.970
I think you could use something similar,

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:03.550
like a sharp pointy knife.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:07.520
If the tooth broke off
and it was still there,

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:11.650
and now he has less of a
leverage to use the skate

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:15.050
to take out the tooth, so
if it was partially broken

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:17.050
and then infection would still be there.

00:16:18.201 --> 00:16:19.034
[man thudding]

00:16:19.034 --> 00:16:19.867
He passed out at the end. I would imagine

00:16:19.867 --> 00:16:23.760
the pain is pretty strong;
you can probably pass out

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:24.810
from that kind of pain.

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:27.800
No, no. Dental procedures at home,

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:29.480
definitely not recommended.

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:31.010
7 out of 10.

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:34.820
I think it's a reasonable
attempt, given his circumstances.

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:37.203
Dislocated my shoulder, though. Hold me.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.040
Most of the shoulder dislocations

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:43.190
are anterior dislocations,
meaning the shoulder joint comes

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:46.410
out to the front, and then
you want to mimic the injury

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:48.070
to be able to put back in.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:49.830
So there's a lot of pulling down

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:53.560
and then kind of putting
it back into the socket.

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:55.560
You need some countertraction,

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:58.660
so I think it helps to
have somebody either pull,

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:01.080
or for you to have
something to push against

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:03.208
before you put the shoulder back in.

00:17:03.208 --> 00:17:05.920
[shoulder clicking]

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:06.920
It's better.

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:08.970
There's actually a very wide range

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:11.560
of presentations when it
comes to shoulder dislocation.

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:12.780
It is one of the common things

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:14.420
that we see in the emergency room.

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:17.090
As many people know, sometimes people get

00:17:17.090 --> 00:17:19.280
shoulder dislocations frequently,

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:21.020
and they're able to kind of pop it back

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:23.660
in themselves or it
spontaneously goes back in,

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:25.580
and they never have to
come to the hospital.

00:17:25.580 --> 00:17:28.750
In some cases, whether
it's someone's first time

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:30.789
or it's just not going
back in, it stays out

00:17:30.789 --> 00:17:33.480
and it can actually be
pretty uncomfortable.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.060
And, you know, everyone
tolerance of pain is different.

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:40.052
Certain patients, they can use their arm

00:17:40.052 --> 00:17:41.180
normally right away.

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:43.090
For them, it happens a lot, frequently,

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:44.390
and they can kind of deal with it.

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:45.470
And for some people, we put them

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:47.610
in a sling and we tell them, you know,

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:51.290
don't move it until you can
go see your orthopedic doctor.

00:17:51.290 --> 00:17:55.100
She's strong, and she has
good control of her body,

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:58.090
so she probably knows what
she can do and can't do.

00:17:58.090 --> 00:18:01.544
I would give it a 4 out of 10,

00:18:01.544 --> 00:18:06.200
just because I don't know
if the motion that she used

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:10.560
to put back her shoulder was
necessarily the correct motion.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:11.530
Thank you for watching.

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:12.440
That's all we have.

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:13.860
And if you enjoyed this video,

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:15.610
check out some of the videos above.

